<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    ★ THE TIERED RUNTIME SUITE ★

    This project exists because a declared target framework and an executed one
    are different things, and nothing in a green build distinguishes them.

    It began as a Tier 3 suite: eleven assemblies declared net472/net48 support
    (ADR-002) and not one line had ever EXECUTED there, because every test
    project targeted net10.0 only. That mattered because ADR-002's cost had been
    paid seven times — portable equivalents for IsExternalInit, DateOnly,
    Index/Range, static HashData, [GeneratedRegex], ReadOnlySpan in a
    zero-dependency assembly, and RSASSA-PSS — and Phase 24f spent the benefit
    on the claim that laboratory device hosts still run .NET Framework. A claim
    nobody had run.

    Then it was pointed one level further out, at the question it had answered
    for Tier 3 but not for the matrix as a whole: WHICH of the declared
    frameworks has actually executed anything? The answer was two of five.
    net472 and net8.0 had never run a test either — the suite had been fixed for
    exactly the tier that prompted it and stopped there.

    net6.0 could not be added: xunit.runner.visualstudio 3.1.5 resolves .NET
    Framework assets for it (NU1701). Tier 2 was not merely untested, it was
    untestable with the pinned toolchain — one of the four findings that
    retired it in ADR-038.

    So this runs on EVERY framework the libraries declare, and that is the
    invariant to preserve: $(EdpfLibraryTargetFrameworks), never a hand-written
    list. A hand-written list is what let three frameworks go unexecuted, and
    the consolidation audit's lesson applies here — a rule that names its
    subjects goes stale, a rule that discovers them does not.

    "It compiles" and "it works" differ most on exactly the constructs a
    portable equivalent replaces: culture-sensitive formatting, Substring vs
    Range, instance vs static hash APIs.

    Deliberately NARROW in what it references — only assemblies that reach
    Tier 3 — so a divergence appears as a difference between runs of identical
    assertions rather than as an absence.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      ★ Clearing the inherited SINGULAR property is load-bearing. ★

      tests/Directory.Build.props sets <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
      for every test project. When a csproj then sets the PLURAL
      <TargetFrameworks>, both are defined — and MSBuild resolves that in favour
      of the singular, disabling cross-targeting entirely. The project builds
      net10.0 and nothing else, silently, with no warning.

      That is not hypothetical. This project shipped on 2026-08-03 declaring
      net48;net10.0, and `dotnet build` produced only net10.0. The CI step added
      the same day to prove Tier 3 executes asked for the net48 framework with
      the no-build flag, against an assembly the build had never produced:
      "The test source file ... was not found", exit code 1.

      So the suite created to prove a framework runs, and the CI step created to
      run it, were BOTH broken by the same inherited property — and neither
      failure was visible, because `dotnet test -f net48` WITHOUT the no-build
      flag builds the missing target on demand and passes. That is how it was
      verified, and that is why it looked fine.

      (The flag names are spelled out rather than quoted because a literal
      double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment — this file did not load
      at all until that was fixed.)
    -->
    <TargetFramework />
    <TargetFrameworks>$(EdpfLibraryTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Edpf.Abstractions\Edpf.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Edpf.Core\Edpf.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Edpf.Compatibility\Edpf.Compatibility.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Edpf.Devices\Edpf.Devices.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
